//! Executable rule checks: a generated script, sandboxed and run over an
//! output-format JSON document.
//!
//! # The security boundary
//!
//! A rule script is generated by an LLM from a description that may itself
//! derive from an untrusted document (PLAN.md: "Generated scripts are code
//! the system runs"). This crate is where that gets treated with the
//! seriousness it deserves:
//!
//! - No native Rust function is ever registered on the boa context. The
//!   host API (`walk`, JSON Pointer construction) is plain JavaScript
//!   (see `prelude.js`), so there is no Rust call surface for a script to
//!   reach into at all.
//! - The default module loader is replaced before anything else happens —
//!   see [`sandbox`]'s module doc for the concrete filesystem read it would
//!   otherwise permit.
//! - Every runtime limit boa exposes (loop iterations, recursion depth,
//!   stack size) is set explicitly, never left at boa's own defaults.
//! - A script's result is never trusted on its say-so: every returned JSON
//!   Pointer is resolved against the actual output before a violation is
//!   accepted, and `pass` must agree with the violation list. See
//!   [`contract`].
//!
//! What this crate does **not** solve, stated rather than glossed over: boa
//! gives no interrupt hook and no heap cap, so a script that evades the
//! loop/recursion limits (there is no known way to, but "no known way" is
//! not a proof) runs to completion, and one that asks for more memory than
//! exists aborts the process it runs in. Neither is containable from
//! inside a process, which is why nothing in this crate tries: callers go
//! through `u2s-rules-host`, which runs every script in a worker process
//! it can put a memory ceiling on and kill. See [`budget::ScriptBudget`].
//!
//! # Entry point
//!
//! [`check::run_check`] is the one function the rest of the workspace
//! calls to check a document: a script, an output document, its format's
//! JSON Schema, and a budget in, a trustworthy [`contract::CheckOutcome`]
//! or a named [`contract::BrokenRule`] out. A broken rule is never the
//! run's problem — see PLAN.md's "an overrunning or throwing script fails
//! the rule, not the run". [`check::classify_check_and_fix`] is the total
//! form of the same evaluation, and the one every caller outside this
//! crate actually uses — it additionally verifies a rule's optional
//! `fix_js` against the check's own violations, never trusting a fix's
//! operations any more than a check's pointers (see [`fix::verify_fix`]
//! and [`contract::BrokenFix`]).

mod budget;
mod check;
mod contract;
mod extract;
mod fix;
mod requires;
mod sandbox;

pub use budget::ScriptBudget;
pub use check::{CheckVerdict, CheckedOutcome, FixOutcome, classify_check_and_fix, run_check};
pub use contract::{BrokenFix, BrokenRule, CheckOutcome, RuleViolation};
pub use extract::run_extract;
pub use requires::read_requires;
